// `remove` taking one key and `remove_all` taking `variadic`, over a Map, a MutableMap, a Set and
// a MutableSet. Both names in all four worlds, which is what the 2026-11-15 split restored.
//
// **This was `remove_all_three_worlds` until 2026-10-22**, when `remove` went variadic and
// absorbed that name -- F197/F198 had built it as a separate plural, item 6 and the last of
// Group A. Four worlds now rather than three: `remove_all` never had an immutable `Set` form, so
// "a set without these" took a fold or a `filter`, and the merge handed it over for free; the
// split kept all four.
//
// **The split is a count and a measurement**, both at map.zm's `remove`: no caller in this
// repository or the two that depend on it ever passed `remove` more than one key, and the
// one-element array the variadic collapse built cost 9-43% across every map size up to 65,536
// entries. The singular is also frameless now, which a variadic native cannot be.
//
// Every plural world takes an array, several values or one value -- and *not* none,
// `#[at_least_one]` being part of the arity. The singular takes exactly one, so `remove(m, ks)`
// where `ks` is an array is now a *key* that is an array, not a spread: for a `Map<string, int>`
// that is a type error, and `remove_all` is the name that spreads.
//
// The returns still differ, for the usual reason: the immutable halves answer a new Map or Set,
// the builders answer whether anything was actually removed. The MutableMap builder answered the
// removed `Option<V>` before the merge and answers `bool` with the others now; that is the one
// capability it cost, and it did not come back with the singular -- map.zm's declaration carries
// the account.
//
// An array's `remove` is deliberately absent from both: it takes an *index*, so a plural form
// would be a different operation with shift semantics to define rather than the same one over
// more arguments.
fn main() {
    val m = Map<string, int> { "a": 1, "b": 2, "c": 3 }
    print(remove_all(m, ["a", "b"]))
    print(remove_all(m, "a", "b"))
    print(remove_all(m, "a"))
    print(remove(m, "a"))
    print(len(m))

    val mm = mutable(m)
    print(remove_all(mm, "a", "b"))
    print(snapshot(mm))
    print(remove(mm, "zz"))
    // The answer dropped in statement position, which `#[effectful]` is what makes legal -- and
    // which is the whole of what `discard` was (deleted 2026-11-15, same change).
    remove(mm, "c")
    print(snapshot(mm))

    val s = Set<int> { 1, 2, 3 }
    print(remove_all(s, [1, 2]))
    print(remove(s, 3))

    val ms = mutable(s)
    print(remove_all(ms, [1, 2]))
    print(snapshot(ms))
    print(remove(ms, 9))
    print(remove(ms, 3))
}
